atician resorts to a  simple yet efficient trick  he combines bean samples from different  pots  if all the beans in this pool are good  he no longer has to test each pot  individually  only if there are bitter beans in the  sample must he perform  additional tests the principle can be  applied any time  groups are tested and it can be used to  combat kovitt 19.  in rwanda sets of 10 and 20 samples are  combined and tested simultaneously  if the pool test result is negative all  the subjects in the pool  sample are indicated as not having covit  19.  but if the pooled result is positive  each sample is re-tested  individually the advantages of the  pulling approach is just  to reduce the cost spent today  for the reagents to reduce the  turnaround time  feedback for results and also to test  massively  in the community or also the group which  are just  which are that concerned for the coffee  19. so those are the main advantages for  this  if the virus has spread dramatically  pooled samples test positive too  often and re-testing becomes costly and  time-consuming  so far however africa has low number of  cases compared to western countries  and the method is proving useful there  this is really helpful for the african  countries but not only african countries  even for developing countries since we  developed this and they published the  pulling approach in the nature paper so  we have been  approached by several countries ghana  and kenya are now applying the strategy  to mass testing  inquiries have also come from the us and  in britain  the university of edinburgh is testing  students with this method  oliver radman is a lecturer and  statistic in the department of  mathematics at  imperial college london uh welcome to  our program and before we get into the  nitty-gritty of mathematical modelling  just very very briefly  what exactly is it that you do at your  department  hi thanks for having me today so i'm  part of the imperial  um college london covet 19 r